WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:04.167
-Quest, it's always great to
have you in the guest chair.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:06.067
And, Shawn, welcome
to "The Tonight Show."

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:07.400
I'm happy that you're here.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.968
You wrote "The Rhythm of Time"
together, right here.

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:14.267
Congratu-- It is officially
a New York Times Best Seller.

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:16.367
Whoo-whoo-whoo-whoo-whoo!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:17.901
-Congrats.
-Thank you.

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:18.934
-Thank you, thank you,
thank you.

00:00:18.934 --> 00:00:20.701
-Congrats on that.
-Appreciate it.

00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:24.334
Shawn, your other best-selling
book, "Razorblade Tears,"

00:00:24.334 --> 00:00:26.567
by the way,
was also featured on our show.

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:27.868
It was on the
Fallon Summer Read List,

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:31.100
but also Barack Obama's
Summer Reading List.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.133
-Yeah.

00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:35.467
-And it's being made
into a movie,

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:36.701
produced
by Jerry Bruckheimer.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:38.400
-Yeah, which is terrifying.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.133
-I mean, have you
processed all of this?

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.701
-Not at all. Not a bit, no.

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:44.534
It's like five years ago, I was
working at a hardware store,

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:46.901
cleaning up spills, and now
the president knows my name.

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:48.133
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:50.834
-That's what's up.
-That's what it's all about.

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:53.033
-Which is crazy,
because the book --

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:54.634
You know, it's like
having him read it,

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:55.968
it's sort of like your mom
reading it, too,

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:57.934
because of like, "Just pass over
the curse words.

00:00:57.934 --> 00:00:59.334
There's terrible stuff that --"

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:02.267
-"No, I'm much more
polite than this."

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.901
-Yeah. Yeah, exactly.
-You crushed it.

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:06.200
And did you guys know
each other before doing work --

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.334
-Actually, you know what?

00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:09.501
I became aware of him when --

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:11.734
Because I follow
your Summer Reading List.

00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:13.334
Assuming that you're going
to do one this year.

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:14.334
-Yeah, we're going
to do one this year.

00:01:14.334 --> 00:01:15.934
Absolutely. Yeah.

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:17.934
Maybe we'll have
"The Rhythm of Time" on the...

00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:19.200
-Hey.
-I mean, I'm just sayin'.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.868
-Just sayin'.

00:01:20.868 --> 00:01:23.334
You guys have -- You both
have written books together.

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:25.467
I mean, not together,
but separately.

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:26.567
But never a kids' book.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:29.601
-Yeah.
-How did this all start?

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:32.968
-Basically, you know,
during the pandemic,

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:35.667
I was quarantining
on a farm, and...

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:36.834
-Yeah, I know.

00:01:36.834 --> 00:01:39.367
-...without --
a farm, without cable.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.868
So, you know,
there was nothing to do,

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.067
only online deejaying.

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.267
Like, we had Wi-Fi.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.267
But I was basically
out of my comfort zone.

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:48.400
And once I realized, like --

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.234
-Because you need
something to do.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:51.734
-Yeah.
Just need something to do,

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:53.834
and I have to do
something creative.

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:59.167
And so I guess at the time
when I was online deejaying

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:04.133
and working on "Summer of Soul"
and then doing the other book,

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.934
"Music is History,"
the people I was staying with,

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:09.100
they're a bunch of life coaches,
and they were like,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.667
"Yo, you're writing another
boring history book about music?

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.234
Like, do something else."

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:15.234
And so they kind
of challenged me.

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.367
I always wanted to write
a time-travel book,

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:20.300
but I'm a nerd
that really doesn't know

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.434
much, like,
science-fiction jargon,

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.367
so I always talked myself
out of it.

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.367
And I guess, like, my publisher
made a playdate with us.

00:02:27.367 --> 00:02:28.501
-Yeah.
-And so --

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:29.901
-That kind of worries --

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:31.300
What time-travel stuff
are you into?

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.033
What do you mean
you're into time travel?

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:34.334
-I love time travel.

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:36.200
Remember I told you
I watched, like, "Palm Springs,"

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.067
like at least 10 times
over the pandemic

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.234
and, you know --

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:40.501
-"Back to the Future"?
-Yeah, all those --

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:41.801
-Oh, yeah, we've had
"Back to the Future" talks.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:43.234
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:45.567
But, so. you're like, "Okay,
I'm going to write this book.

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:48.234
I'll see if I can get
S.A. Cosby to work --

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.434
Do you want to work --"

00:02:49.434 --> 00:02:51.534
Did you think
about it and go like, "Eh..."?

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:52.801
-Well, it was funny,

00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:54.033
because when they contacted me,
it was like,

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.767
"Are you sure you want me
to write a kids' book?"

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.400
Because I write crime novels.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.567
Usually, people getting shot
in the face in my books.

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:02.100
-And crazier stuff than that.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.033
-I don't know if I'm the perfect
person to write a kids' novel.

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:05.667
-I wouldn't say you're
my first choice.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:06.968
-Yeah.
-You're not Dr. Seuss.

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.234
-No, no, no, no.

00:03:08.234 --> 00:03:10.868
Not unless Dr. Seuss hitting
people with shovels.

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:13.968
But once we started talking
about it, it was like, wow.

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:15.467
You know, you get an opportunity
to work with Questlove.

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:18.267
First of all, I'm an old hip-hop
head from back in the day,

00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:20.300
so get to work with The Roots --

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.634
You know, "Things Fall Apart,"
you know, and

00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:24.033
"Do You Want More?!!!??!"

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:25.434
And it's like, so when they
call you, it's like, "Yeah,

00:03:25.434 --> 00:03:27.400
Questlove wants to write
a book with you."

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.734
And I'm like,
"Yeah, alright, whatever.

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:30.167
What do I got to do?"
Where I got to show up? Yeah."

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:31.634
You know, so --

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:35.701
-And how did you choose for it
to be for middle-school kids?

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:38.801
-Alright, so, when our
fourth album came out,

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.100
"Things Fall Apart,"

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.033
a friend of mine
who was a teacher, like,

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:44.367
convinced me, like, you know,
"Come and talk to the kids."

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:45.834
I was like, "Alright,
we have enough notoriety.

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:46.868
Like, I'll come
to the school like,

00:03:46.868 --> 00:03:48.267
'Hey, kids, how you doing?'"

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:51.868
And, basically, the first
question that the kids asked,

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:54.601
like, during Q&amp;A time -- Like,
"Anybody have any questions?",

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.667
they were like --
One kid stood up,

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.734
was like, "Yeah, do you know,
like, a real celebrity,

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:01.634
like André 3000?"

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:03.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:06.701
-Like you're not a real -- Yeah.
"Do you know a real celebrity?"

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:11.334
-Yeah. And so inside,
I was just like, "Them kids."

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.934
-Kids are harsh, man.

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:14.467
-Yeah, so, I was just like,

00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.033
"Alright, let me just
stay away from kids."

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.968
Like, so that kind of
discouraged me from --

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:20.567
That was, like,
such a humiliating moment

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:22.167
that I let that sit
with me forever.

00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:23.300
-Yeah.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.234
-You know, I think
the pandemic is about,

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:29.534
you know, we're either going to
pivot or we're going to panic.

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:33.167
And so, pretty much,
I decided, like, to make a list

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:34.801
of everything
that I had a fear of

00:04:34.801 --> 00:04:37.667
and just, one by one,
just rewrite the --

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:40.167
-Check them off the list.
-Yeah, rewrite the narrative.

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:42.667
So, I always wanted
to write a kids' book.

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:44.100
And so -- And, plus,
I got tired of you

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.801
writing all the kids' books.

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:48.901
Every time I turn around,
you're in the

00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:50.601
New York Times Best Sellers.

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:51.868
-So now you got to be --

00:04:51.868 --> 00:04:53.501
-Every time, you do it like
that, I was like, "Aah!"

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.801
-"Aah, I got to do --" Come on.
-Right.

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:58.167
-It's a great story
and it's well-written.

00:04:58.167 --> 00:05:00.467
And you are on The
New York Times Best Seller list.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:02.634
Congratulations.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:03.767
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:05.367
Which is wild.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:08.367
It's like, you know,
I never thought I'd be

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.367
on New York Times Best Seller
list at all

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:11.934
and that I'd be on there twice.

00:05:11.934 --> 00:05:13.901
It's like, somebody's got to
pinch me, wake me up.

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:15.801
-Yeah, come on, bud.

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:17.234
But, I mean, it's also --

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:18.901
It's a whole new
generation of --

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:20.734
Now you got -- Really, this is
the toughest audience.

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:23.067
-They are. They are.
-They're honest with you.

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:24.400
If they don't like it,
they don't like it.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.901
-I went to talk to some kids
a couple weeks ago

00:05:25.901 --> 00:05:28.234
at a middle school,
and it was like the first

00:05:28.234 --> 00:05:30.734
5, 10 minutes, it's terrifying.

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:32.434
It's just like
you're staring at them,

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:34.501
they're staring at you,
and it's like,

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:35.567
"Somebody blink first."

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:36.667
-Yeah.

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.767
-Somebody do something.
Yeah, exactly.

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:39.901
-Yeah.

00:05:39.901 --> 00:05:42.934
-Well, I think what
has to happen now is you

00:05:42.934 --> 00:05:44.601
have to write a sequel.

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:48.901
-Well, they're already --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:48.901 --> 00:05:50.601
-That's what happens
when you're successful.

00:05:50.601 --> 00:05:52.334
-See, I thought, in the
pandemic, it was going to be

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.601
a one-and-done thing.

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.234
And, you know,
a lot of this book

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:57.300
borrows heavy from
my life, so --

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.133
Remember when you told me
the first week of the show,

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:00.801
like, everything that we ever --

00:06:00.801 --> 00:06:02.200
You said Letterman told you,
like, everything

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.467
that was ever in your life,

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:04.701
you're going to use
for this show.

00:06:04.701 --> 00:06:05.801
-Yeah.

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:07.934
-Like, I gave everything
for this one book,

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:09.634
and then, you know,
they're like,

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.634
"You got to write two more."

00:06:11.634 --> 00:06:13.501
-Yeah. Now --

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:15.334
-But we're talking
about the second book.

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:16.868
-Now you got
to get real creative, yeah.

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:19.334
-Working on it. We're going
to dig up some stuff.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:23.300
-It's so fun and it's very you,
because I know you.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.534
-Yeah.
-And it's very well-done.

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:27.634
Congratulations to both of you,
and thank you very much.

00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:29.934
-Thank you so much. Thank you.
-Thank you.

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:32.000
-Questlove, S.A. Cosby,
everybody.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.901
"The Rhythm of Time" is out now.

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:36.400
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.801
Stick around, everybody!

